A controller or processor must designate a data protection officer in these cases:  
- where the processing is carried out by a public authority or body, except courts acting in their judicial capacity [1];  
- where the core activities consist of processing operations that, by their nature, scope, and/or purposes, require regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects on a large scale [1];  
- where the core activities consist of processing on a large scale of special categories of data under Article 9 and personal data relating to criminal convictions and offences under Article 10 [1].

The documents also say that outside those cases, a controller or processor, or associations and other bodies representing them, may designate a data protection officer, and must do so where Union or Member State law requires it [2].

The documents do not cover any additional criteria beyond those listed in Article 37.

Under DORA Article 30, financial entities must include contractual provisions that clearly allocate the rights and obligations of both parties in writing, with the full contract and service level agreements in one written document available in a durable, downloadable, and accessible format [1].

At a minimum, the arrangement must include:
- a clear and complete description of all functions and ICT services to be provided, including whether subcontracting is permitted and on what conditions [1];
- the locations where services and data processing/storage will take place, and advance notice if those locations change [1];
- provisions on availability, authenticity, integrity, and confidentiality for data protection, including personal data [1];
- conditions for ICT third-party service providers’ participation in ICT security awareness programmes and digital operational resilience training [2].

For ICT services supporting critical or important functions, additional provisions are required, including:
- full service level descriptions with updates/revisions and precise quantitative and qualitative performance targets for monitoring and corrective action [2];
- notice periods and reporting obligations, including notification of any development materially affecting the provider’s ability to deliver services [2];
- requirements to implement and test business contingency plans and maintain appropriate ICT security measures, tools, and policies [2];
- access, recovery, and return of personal and non-personal data in an easily accessible format if the provider becomes insolvent, is resolved, discontinues business, or the contract ends [4];
- the obligation to provide assistance to the financial entity at no additional cost, or at a pre-agreed cost, when an ICT incident occurs [4];
- full cooperation with competent and resolution authorities [4];
- termination rights and minimum notice periods [4].

The rules also say financial entities should consider using standard contractual clauses developed by public authorities [3].

What these passages do not fully cover: they do not provide the complete detailed list of all possible contractual requirements beyond the excerpts shown, and they do not explain every implementation detail of the RTS referenced in Article 30(5) [3].
